From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: codecs: lpass-macro: depend on the DSP that provides the clocks

Restart the audio DSP on an x1e80100 and every LPASS macro codec is left
permanently broken:

    # echo stop > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state
    # echo start > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state
    clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_HW_DCODEC
    clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_HW_MACRO
    va_macro 6d44000.codec: unable to prepare mclk

    # cat /sys/bus/platform/devices/6d44000.codec/power/runtime_status
    error

All four macros end up there, the card survives but is silent in both
directions, and only a reboot clears it.

Every clock these codecs use -- mclk, the HW_MACRO and HW_DCODEC votes, and
the fsgen they pass between themselves -- comes from &q6prmcc, a clock
controller on a GPR service of the audio DSP. Restarting the DSP therefore
unregisters the provider while the codecs still hold its clocks.

Nothing tells them it is about to happen. clk_get() records no dependency, and
the device links fw_devlink builds from the "clocks" phandle point at the
remoteproc rather than at the service, because the GPR service devices do not
exist yet when the tree is parsed -- and the remoteproc never unbinds, so the
core sees no supplier change. clk_unregister() consequently runs with live
consumers, and rather than failing it installs clk_nodrv_ops on every handle,
whose .prepare returns -ENXIO until the last clk_put(). The next
va_macro_runtime_resume() fails there, rpm_callback() latches the errno in
power.runtime_error, and the codec can no longer be resumed at all:
pm_runtime_get_sync() returns -EINVAL without invoking the callback, which is
the -EINVAL that surfaces from snd_soc_pcm_component_pm_runtime_get().

There is no recovery after the fact. Reviving a handle requires a clk_put()
and a fresh clk_get(), so a re-probe -- and re-probing at that point means
tearing the codec, SoundWire and card stack down while every clock in it is
already clk_nodrv_ops, which hangs the machine.

So record the dependency instead, on the device that actually supplies the
clocks. __device_release_driver() unbinds consumers before it calls the
supplier's remove callback, so the codecs release live clocks on the way down
and clk_unregister() then finds no consumers left; DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER
has the core probe them again when the DSP returns. No dead handle is ever
created, nothing touches an unclocked LPASS register, and no runtime PM error
is latched.

Platforms whose macros take their clocks from an always-on block resolve no
packet-router device and are left alone.

Not hibernation-specific: a plain SSR reproduces it, and the hibernate path
merely performs a stop/start.

---
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-macro-common.h
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-macro-common.h
@@ -39,6 +39,7 @@
 
 struct lpass_macro *lpass_macro_pds_init(struct device *dev);
 void lpass_macro_pds_exit(struct lpass_macro *pds);
+int lpass_macro_link_clk_provider(struct device *dev);
 void lpass_macro_set_codec_version(enum lpass_codec_version version);
 enum lpass_codec_version lpass_macro_get_codec_version(void);
 
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-macro-common.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-macro-common.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/pm_domain.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
+#include <linux/soc/qcom/apr.h>
 
 #include "lpass-macro-common.h"
 
@@ -58,6 +59,76 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(lpass_macro_pds_init);
 
+/**
+ * lpass_macro_link_clk_provider() - depend on the DSP that provides our clocks
+ * @dev: the macro codec device
+ *
+ * On these SoCs every clock a macro codec uses -- mclk, the HW_MACRO and
+ * HW_DCODEC votes, and fsgen -- comes from a clock controller that lives on
+ * the audio DSP's packet-router bus, so the provider is torn down whenever the
+ * DSP is restarted. An SSR does it, and so does the stop/start a hibernation
+ * cycle performs.
+ *
+ * Nothing tells the codec that is about to happen. clk_get() records no
+ * dependency, and the device links fw_devlink builds from the "clocks" phandle
+ * point at the remoteproc -- the nearest ancestor that had a struct device
+ * when the tree was parsed -- which never unbinds. So clk_unregister() runs
+ * with this driver still holding its clocks, and rather than failing it gives
+ * every one of them clk_nodrv_ops, whose .prepare returns -ENXIO for good.
+ * The next runtime resume fails, rpm_callback() latches the errno in
+ * power.runtime_error, and the codec is stuck in runtime PM error with no way
+ * back: recovering a handle needs a clk_put() and a fresh clk_get(), which
+ * only a re-probe performs.
+ *
+ * Link to the device that really supplies the clocks instead.
+ * __device_release_driver() unbinds consumers before it calls the supplier's
+ * remove callback, so the codec releases *live* clocks on the way down, and
+ * DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER has the core probe it again once the DSP is back.
+ *
+ * Absent or non-DSP providers are not an error: platforms whose macros take
+ * their clocks from an always-on block have nothing to depend on here.
+ */
+int lpass_macro_link_clk_provider(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct device_node *provider, *service;
+	struct device_link *link;
+	struct device *supplier;
+
+	provider = of_parse_phandle(dev->of_node, "clocks", 0);
+	if (!provider)
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * The provider is a clock-controller child node of the packet-router
+	 * service; the service is what owns a struct device.
+	 */
+	service = of_get_parent(provider);
+	of_node_put(provider);
+	if (!service)
+		return 0;
+
+	supplier = bus_find_device_by_of_node(&aprbus, service);
+	of_node_put(service);
+	if (!supplier)
+		return 0;
+
+	link = device_link_add(dev, supplier, DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER);
+	put_device(supplier);
+	if (!link) {
+		/*
+		 * Not fatal. Without the link this codec behaves as it did
+		 * before: it works until the DSP is restarted and is then
+		 * stuck in runtime PM error. That is strictly better than
+		 * refusing to probe and leaving the card with no codecs.
+		 */
+		dev_warn(dev, "no link to the clock provider; a DSP restart will break this codec\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(lpass_macro_link_clk_provider);
+
 void lpass_macro_pds_exit(struct lpass_macro *pds)
 {
 	if (pds) {
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-va-macro.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-va-macro.c
@@ -1558,6 +1558,10 @@
 	if (IS_ERR(va->mclk))
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(va->mclk), "unable to get mclk clock\n");
 
+	ret = lpass_macro_link_clk_provider(dev);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	va->pds = lpass_macro_pds_init(dev);
 	if (IS_ERR(va->pds))
 		return PTR_ERR(va->pds);
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-rx-macro.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/lpass-rx-macro.c
@@ -3786,6 +3786,10 @@
 	if (IS_ERR(rx->mclk))
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(rx->mclk), "unable to get mclk clock\n");
 
+	ret = lpass_macro_link_clk_provider(dev);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	if (flags & LPASS_MACRO_FLAG_HAS_NPL_CLOCK) {
 		rx->npl = devm_clk_get(dev, "npl");
 		if (IS_ERR(rx->npl))
